## 25. TESTING AND PHYSICAL VALIDATION

Implement automated unit tests for the physics engine.

Test at least the following:

ELECTRIC FIELD:

* Verify that doubling the distance from an isolated point charge reduces field magnitude to one-quarter under the same medium conditions.
* Verify superposition of fields from multiple charges.
* Verify the field direction for positive and negative charges.

ELECTRIC FLUX:

* Verify maximum positive flux when the field aligns with the surface normal.
* Verify zero flux when the field is parallel to the surface.
* Verify the sign reversal when the normal direction is reversed.

ELECTRIC FLUX DENSITY:

* Verify D = epsilon E for the selected linear dielectric model.

MAGNETIC FIELD:

* Verify the inverse-distance relationship for an ideal infinite straight wire.
* Verify the field direction using a consistent right-hand rule.
* Verify the linear relationship between H and B in the selected linear material.

MAGNETIC FLUX:

* Verify maximum flux when the field aligns with the surface normal.
* Verify zero flux when the field is parallel to the surface.
* Verify flux linkage scales with the number of turns when every turn has the same flux.

INDUCTION:

* Verify induced voltage from the numerical derivative of flux linkage.
* Verify polarity reversal when flux-change direction reverses.
* Verify zero induced voltage for constant flux linkage.
* Verify current response in the selected circuit model.

RL CIRCUIT:

* Verify the correct time constant.
* Verify the steady-state current for a DC-driven ideal series RL circuit with positive resistance.
* Verify the initial inductor current cannot change instantaneously in the ideal model.

TRANSFORMER:

* Verify the ideal turns ratio.
* Verify the ideal power relationship under consistent voltage and current conventions.

ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES:

* Verify v = f lambda.
* Verify wavelength changes inversely with frequency when propagation speed is fixed.

NUMERICAL METHODS:

* Verify convergence against analytical solutions.
* Test singularities and invalid input.
* Test numerical stability.
* Test unit consistency.

Add additional tests for every simulation.

Do not claim that the application is physically validated merely because its animations look realistic.

